## Begin with the relationship

An article has an author. That sounds obvious, but the way we represent that relationship determines how much work the next version will need.

If a name and biography are copied into every article, updating an author means editing many records. A stable reference, such as `authorId`, makes that relationship explicit.

```ts
type ArticleSource = {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  authorId: string;
  status: 'draft' | 'published';
  content: string;
};
```

The storage can still be local files. A useful model does not require a database.

## Put the public boundary in one place

A listing page, a related-articles section, and a direct article route all need the same answer to a basic question: *is this content public?*

Apply that rule before content reaches the page. A shared content repository gives every public view the same collection to work with.

```ts
const publicArticles = articles.filter(
  (article) => article.status === 'published'
);
```

This example shows the status rule only. A real publication may also exclude future publication dates and validate the author reference before generating a page.

## Keep the interface focused

A card needs a title, an excerpt, a cover, and enough context to help someone choose what to read. It does not need the full body of every article in its page data.

- Fetch complete content for the reading page.
- Pass summaries to discovery views.
- Keep storage access out of reusable components.
- Validate metadata close to the content source.

![Three connected layers representing content, queries, and presentation.](/assets/images/journal/architecture.png "Local content → shared queries → public views")

## Design for the next change you can name

Separating authors from articles is useful now and later. Introducing an entire permissions system before there are accounts is a different kind of work.

The aim is a clear boundary: today, files supply the content; later, another source can supply the same shape. The public interface should not need to know how the writing was stored.

---

A small model with explicit relationships often leaves more room to grow than a large model full of speculative features.
